    "q-352629": {
      "dom_id": "step-q352629",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{2{sin}^{3}⁡x}{1-{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "We use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x=1\\,⟹\\,1-{cos}^{2}⁡x={sin}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{2{sin}^{3}⁡x}{1-{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-2[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})] \\\\  & =-2\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
