      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}x(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x){cos}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "In this case, we have to use the identity  $1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x.$ Substituting this into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}x(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x){cos}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}x{sec}^{2}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{32}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
