    "q-49323": {
      "dom_id": "step-q49323",
      "name": "Question 16",
      "problem": "Calculate $\\int \\frac{{cos}^{3}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{{sin}^{2}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})-1}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "From the identity  ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1$ with $\\theta =x+\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})+{sin}^{2}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})-1 & =-{cos}^{2}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{cos}^{3}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{{sin}^{2}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{{cos}^{3}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{-{cos}^{2}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
