    "q-114468": {
      "dom_id": "step-q114468",
      "name": "Question 17",
      "problem": "Calculate ${\\int }_{1}^{2}(2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}-1)-3+2{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}-1))\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "From the identity ${sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ with $\\theta =\\frac{x}{3}-1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}-1)+{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}-1)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}-1)-3+2{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}-1))\\text{d}x & = \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{2}(2[{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}-1)+{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}-1)]-3)\\text{d}x & = \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{2}(2(1)-3)\\text{d}x & = \\\\ -{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\text{d}x & = \\\\ -x\\,{|}_{1}^{2} & = \\\\ -(2-1) & = \\\\ -1 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
